**Repro** (zod 4.4.3, also present on `main`):

```ts
import * as z from "zod";

let calls = 0;
const codec = z.codec(z.iso.datetime(), z.date(), {
  decode: (iso) => new Date(iso),
  encode: (date) => {
    calls++;
    return date.toISOString();
  },
});

z.encode(z.object({ at: codec }), { at: new Date() });
// calls === 1 ✅

calls = 0;
z.encode(z.object({ at: codec }).refine(() => true), { at: new Date() });
// calls === 2 ❌ — one checked ancestor doubles it

calls = 0;
const inner = z.object({ at: codec }).refine(() => true);
z.encode(z.object({ nested: inner }).refine(() => true), { nested: { at: new Date() } });
// calls === 3 ❌ — multiplies with nesting depth

calls = 0;
z.encode(z.object({ items: z.array(z.object({ at: codec })).min(1) }), {
  items: [{ at: new Date() }, { at: new Date() }],
});
// calls === 4 ❌ — twice per element
```

### Cause

In the backward direction, `$ZodType`'s `run` performs a canary parse with `skipChecks: true` (shielding domain-side checks from invalid values), then — after this node's checks — re-parses the value in full (`handleCanaryResult` in `packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts`). `skipChecks` skips *checks*, but not *transforms*: every codec `encode` and pipe transform in the subtree executes once per pass, so each check-bearing ancestor adds a full extra execution of the subtree below it.

### Impact

- Side-effectful or non-idempotent `encode` functions (metrics, ID allocation, logging) fire 2–N times per `encode()` call.
- Expensive encodes (encryption, compression, big payloads) pay 2x+ wall time whenever any ancestor carries a `.refine()`/`.min()`/etc.
- A related reporting inconsistency: because the canary skips checks and aborts on the first fatal issue, continuable check issues that `parse()` reports are masked in `encode()` for the same schema and value:

```ts
const schema = z.object({ a: z.string().min(5), b: z.number() }).refine(() => true);
z.safeParse(schema, { a: "abc", b: "nan" }).error.issues;
// [too_small at ["a"], invalid_type at ["b"]]
z.safeEncode(schema, { a: "abc", b: "nan" }).error.issues;
// [invalid_type at ["b"]] — too_small masked
```

### Suggested fix

The second parse is only needed when the node's **own checks can mutate the value** — and `$ZodCheckOverwrite` is the designated mutating check family (`trim`, `toLowerCase`, `toUpperCase`, `normalize`, `slugify` all build on it). So: run the canary **with** descendant checks enabled; when the node's own checks contain no overwrite-family check, reuse the canary's value and issues and elide the second parse. Overwrite-bearing nodes keep the current two-pass flow, and abort semantics are unchanged (an invalid value still returns before this node's checks execute).

Working implementation: [`drudolf/zod@encode-canary-reuse`](https://github.com/drudolf/zod/tree/encode-canary-reuse), single commit [`154494f`](https://github.com/drudolf/zod/commit/154494fb54670e315dcc0f83122493c504f5166e). All 3,821 existing tests pass, plus 10 new tests in `codec.test.ts` covering encode-once semantics, descendant-check failures under checked parents (sync/async), issue ordering, overwrite parents, and unidirectional transforms.

Measured: ~1.9x faster encode on check-dense codec schemas (µbench, output verified byte-identical); +4–10% on realistic mixed schemas in fastify serialization benchmarks.

Two deliberate behavioral notes:

1. An aborted canary now also reports continuable descendant check issues — this aligns backward reporting with forward (fixes the masking shown above).
2. A custom check that mutates `payload.value` without being an overwrite check would lose its mutation in the reuse path (previously the re-parse picked it up). `.overwrite()` is the designated mutation mechanism; runtime value-identity detection was rejected as unsound for in-place reference mutation.

Happy to open a PR from the branch if this direction is acceptable.

## Description

`z.iso.datetime()` rejects ISO 8601 datetime strings using the expanded year representation (leading `+`/`-` sign with a 6-digit year).

## Reproduction

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.iso.datetime();

const date1 = new Date("9999-12-31T00:00:00.000Z").toISOString();
console.log(date1); // "9999-12-31T00:00:00.000Z"
console.log(schema.safeParse(date1)); // { success: true, data: '9999-12-31T00:00:00.000Z' }

const date2 = new Date("+010000-01-01T00:00:00.000Z").toISOString();
console.log(date2); // "+010000-01-01T00:00:00.000Z"
console.log(schema.safeParse(date2));
// { success: false, error: ZodError: [{ code: "invalid_format", format: "datetime", message: "Invalid ISO datetime", ... }] }
```

## Expected behavior

Both `safeParse` calls should succeed, since both inputs are valid outputs of `Date#toISOString()`.

Per the [docs](https://zod.dev/api?id=iso-datetimes#iso-datetimes):

> The `z.iso.datetime()` method enforces ISO 8601;

ISO 8601 permits an expanded year representation (leading `+`/`-` sign with a 6-digit year) for years outside 0000–9999.

Rejecting a valid ISO 8601 expanded-year string is a mismatch between the documented behavior and the implementation.

## Actual behavior

The year-10000 case fails with `Invalid ISO datetime`.

## Additional context

