Code below shows a minimal repro of the issue.  The type-checker is not able to understand that `z.infer<typeof SCHEMA_MAP[T]>` and `SCHEMA_MAP[T].parse(data)` have compatible types.  Using a direct index to the type internals via `(typeof SCHEMA_MAP[T])["_zod"]["output"]` does recognize a compatible type.  This seems like a bug with `z.infer` and appeared as a new issue when we upgraded to v4.

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

enum SchemaTypes {
    A,
    B,
}

const SCHEMA_MAP = {
    [SchemaTypes.A]: z.string(),
    [SchemaTypes.B]: z.number(),
};

export function parseDataForType<T extends SchemaTypes>(
    type: T,
    data: unknown,
): z.infer<(typeof SCHEMA_MAP)[T]> {
    // Has TS Error: Type string | number is not assignable to type output<{ 0: ZodString; 1: ZodNumber }[T]>
    return SCHEMA_MAP[type].parse(data);
}

// No TS error
export function parseDataForType2<T extends SchemaTypes>(
    type: T,
    data: unknown,
): (typeof SCHEMA_MAP)[T]["_zod"]["output"] {
    return SCHEMA_MAP[type].parse(data);
}

// No return type, use TS inference
export function parseDataForType3<T extends SchemaTypes>(type: T, data: unknown) {
    return SCHEMA_MAP[type].parse(data);
}

const a = parseDataForType(SchemaTypes.A, "foo"); // a = string
const a2 = parseDataForType2(SchemaTypes.A, "foo"); // a = string
const a3 = parseDataForType3(SchemaTypes.A, "foo"); // a = string | number

const b = parseDataForType(SchemaTypes.B, 123); // b = number
const b2 = parseDataForType2(SchemaTypes.B, 123); // b2 = number
const b3 = parseDataForType3(SchemaTypes.B, 123); // b3 = string | number
```

### Description
Chaining `.refine()` downstream of a `.transform()` results in the refinement check being completely skipped if any primitive validation error occurs upstream in the base schema. 

While this prevents running custom refinement logic on invalid/undefined transformation objects, it introduces a breaking change from previous versions by truncating the error collection path. Cross-field validation errors that rely on transformed types are entirely dropped rather than aggregated alongside base field issues.

