I need to apply `.strict()` based on some condition so I use a wrapper like this:

```typescript
const strictIfTesting = <T extends z.ZodObject>(schema: T) => {
    const isProductionEnvironment = "window" in globalThis
    return isProductionEnvironment ? schema : schema.strict() as T
}
```

The problem is in `as T` cast which is just an ugly workaround to keep typing works.

This works:

```typescript
const mySchema = () => strictIfTesting(z.object({
    someKey: strictIfTesting(z.object({
        anotherKey: z.string().nonempty().nonoptional()
    })).nonoptional()
}))

const strictIfTesting = <T extends z.ZodObject>(schema: T) => {
    const isProductionEnvironment = "window" in globalThis
    return isProductionEnvironment ? schema : schema.strict() as T
}

const myObject = mySchema().parse({})
console.log(myObject.someKey.anotherKey) // Everything works fine
```

But this is not:

```typescript
const mySchema = () => strictIfTesting(z.object({
    someKey: strictIfTesting(z.object({
        anotherKey: z.string().nonempty().nonoptional()
    })).nonoptional()
}))

const strictIfTesting = <T extends z.ZodObject>(schema: T) => {
    const isProductionEnvironment = "window" in globalThis
    return isProductionEnvironment ? schema : schema.strict()
}

const myObject = mySchema().parse({})
console.log(myObject.someKey.anotherKey) // Not compiles: myObject.someKey' is of type 'unknown'
```

Everything works as expected if abandon the `strictIfTesting()` in favor of the direct usage of the `.strict()`:

```typescript
const mySchema = () => z.object({
    someKey: z.object({
        anotherKey: z.string().nonempty().nonoptional()
    }).strict().nonoptional()
}).strict()

const myObject = mySchema().parse({})
console.log(myObject.someKey.anotherKey) // Everything works fine
```

So it's clear that I do something wrong defining types of `strictIfTesting()`. How to implement conditional strict validation without ugly workarounds?

Several exported members are deprecated with JSDocs but those deprecations are not communicated properly. IDEs do not show deprecation messages in tooltips and do not display the deprecated member with a strikethrough.

For example, `z.ZodTypeAny` is deprecated in v4, but shows normally in VSCode and Cursor. Following the definition takes us directly to the definition of `z.ZodType`. It is only if we happen to look at v4/classic/compat.ts do we see
```ts
export type {
  /** @deprecated Use z.ZodType (without generics) instead. */
  ZodType as ZodTypeAny,
  /** @deprecated Use `z.ZodType` */
  ZodType as ZodSchema,
  /** @deprecated Use `z.ZodType` */
  ZodType as Schema,
};
```

Members that are re-exported in this way lose their `@deprecated` tag and implementers only see the deprecated member as an alias for the member they link to.

I have a simple PR ready, but the contributing guide suggests I make an issue first to get feedback/approval.

When a schema with `meta({ id })` is passed directly as the root to `z.toJSONSchema()`, the `id` is silently dropped instead of being hoisted into `$defs` with a root-level `$ref`:

```typescript
const User = z.object({ name: z.string() }).meta({ id: 'User' });

z.toJSONSchema(z.object({ User }));
// correctly emits $defs.User + $ref

z.toJSONSchema(User);
// id is dropped, no $defs, no $ref
```

Expected output for the root case:

```json
{
  "$ref": "#/$defs/User",
  "$defs": {
    "User": { "type": "object", "properties": { "name": { "type": "string" } } }
  }
}
```

### Why this matters

In [oRPC](https://orpc.dev/), users define procedures with `.input` and `.output` and can attach an `id` to their schemas for OpenAPI generation. This is the only issue blocking oRPC v2 from using Zod's built-in `toJSONSchema` instead of maintaining a custom conversion layer.

```typescript
const UserSchema = z.object({ name: z.string() }).meta({ id: 'User' });

const procedure = orpc.input(UserSchema).output(UserSchema).handler(...);
``

## Human Notes

I was helped very much by Claude in identifying this issue and preparing it to be reported.  But I am quite confident of the validity of the issue, reproducibility, and the necessity to correct the issue at the zod level.  Our team will be moving ahead with a local zod patch.  But having production code quietly breaking in over a dozen places when we added OTEL is something I wouldn't want others to go through.

## Description

When `globalThis.Promise` has been replaced by a subclass (Zone.js's `ZoneAwarePromise`, Angular's NgZone, `@opentelemetry/context-zone`, etc.), zod v4 silently drops issues added inside an `async` `superRefine`. `parseAsync` resolves "successfully" before the async refinement even completes, and any `ctx.addIssue()` call inside the refinement has no effect on the parse result.

This breaks form validation in any app that uses zod async refinements alongside browser tracing/context libraries. We hit this in production via `@opentelemetry/context-zone` and the user-facing impact was significant — a duplicate-email check in an admin form silently let through submissions despite the async refinement detecting them, which caused 500 errors downstream.

## Minimum reproduction

Repro repo: https://github.com/Restingsound/zod-async-refine-zonejs-repro — also pasted below.

```js
// repro.mjs
import "zone.js"; // patches globalThis.Promise = ZoneAwarePromise
import { z } from "zod";

console.log("globalThis.Promise.name:", globalThis.Promise.name);
console.log(
  "async-fn return instanceof globalThis.Promise:",
  ((async () => {})() instanceof globalThis.Promise),
);

const schema = z
  .object({ email: z.string() })
  .superRefine(async (data, ctx) => {
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 10));
    ctx.addIssue({
      code: "custom",
      message: "Always fails",
      path: ["email"],
    });
  });

const result = await schema.safeParseAsync({ email: "test@example.com" });
console.log("result.success:", result.success);
console.log("result.error?.issues:", result.error?.issues);
```

```json
// package.json
{
  "name": "repro",
  "private": true,
  "type": "module",
  "dependencies": {
    "zod": "4.4.3",
    "zone.js": "0.16.2"
  }
}
```

Run: `pnpm i && node repro.mjs`

### Expected output

```
result.success: false
result.error?.issues: [ { code: 'custom', message: 'Always fails', path: ['email'] } ]
```

### Actual output

```
globalThis.Promise.name: ZoneAwarePromise
async-fn return instanceof globalThis.Promise: false   ← root cause
result.success: true
result.error?.issues: undefined
```

A control script that omits `import "zone.js"` produces the expected output, confirming this is specifically the patched-Promise interaction.

## Root cause

In [`packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts`](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod) (`$ZodType.init` → `runChecks`), the async-vs-sync branch hinges on:

```js
const _ = ch._zod.check(payload);
if (_ instanceof Promise && ctx?.async === false) {
  throw new core.$ZodAsyncError();
}
if (asyncResult || _ instanceof Promise) {
  asyncResult = (asyncResult ?? Promise.resolve()).then(async () => {
    await _;
    // …collect issues added during await
  });
}
```

The fallthrough path (when `_ instanceof Promise` is `false`) returns the payload synchronously without awaiting the refinement's promise. `parseAsync` then sees `result.issues.length === 0` and resolves "successfully," while the user's `await` and subsequent `ctx.addIssue()` actually run a tick later on a Promise no one is waiting on.

The reason this happens under Zone.js is subtle but well-defined:

1. Zone.js sets `globalThis.Promise = ZoneAwarePromise` at load time.
2. ES2017+ `async function` syntax (V8 et al.) constructs its return value from the internal `%Promise%` intrinsic, **not** from `globalThis.Promise`. The returned object is a native `Promise` instance.
3. Inside zod's bundled scope, the bare identifier `Promise` resolves to `globalThis.Promise` (now `ZoneAwarePromise`).
4. `nativePromise instanceof ZoneAwarePromise` → `false`. zod's branch chooses the sync path.

OpenTelemetry's own [`@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-web` README](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-js/tree/main/packages/opentelemetry-sdk-trace-web) explicitly notes:

> `ZoneContextManager` does not work with JS code targeting `ES2017+`. In order to use the `ZoneContextManager`, please transpile back to `ES2015`.

So this isn't a Zone.js bug per se — it's a consequence of patching `globalThis.Promise` in a modern JS environment, which is also what NgZone and any other "context propagation via Promise patch" library will do.

## Suggested fix

Replace `instanceof Promise` with duck-typing on `.then`:

```js
const isThenable = (v) => v != null && typeof v.then === "function";

// …
if (isThenable(_) && ctx?.async === false) {
  throw new core.$ZodAsyncError();
}
if (asyncResult || isThenable(_)) {
  asyncResult = (asyncResult ?? Promise.resolve()).then(async () => {
    await _;
    // …
  });
}
```

This is the idiomatic Promise/A+ approach and is robust to any environment that wraps, subclasses, or replaces `Promise`. It also matches how `await` itself recognizes thenables internally (which is precisely why the async refinement's body runs correctly even though zod misclassifies the return type).

Same pattern would apply at every other `instanceof Promise` site inside zod v4 core (in `packages/core/src/schemas.ts` — `$ZodType.init`'s `runChecks` loop, the pipe/codec result handlers, and `parseAsync` itself).

## Affected environments

- `zone.js` (any version that patches `Promise`)
- Angular apps (NgZone uses Zone.js)
- `@opentelemetry/context-zone` (transitive Zone.js)
- Any custom code that does `globalThis.Promise = SubclassOfPromise` for context tracking

Confirmed broken with: `zod@4.4.3` (latest at time of filing), `zone.js@0.16.2` (latest), Node 22.

## Workarounds (for users hitting this)

Until a fix lands, options are:

1. Move async checks out of `superRefine` into your form's submit handler / `onBlur` callback. Use `setError` (RHF) or equivalent.
2. If using `@opentelemetry/context-zone`, switch to `StackContextManager` from `@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-web` (no Promise patching, but no context propagation across async boundaries either — see OTel docs).
3. Patch zod locally with the duck-typing fix above.

## Problem

Schemas composed from codecs have distinct input and output types. At the type level, `z.input<>` and `z.output<>` give you both sides. But at the value level, there's no way to get a **plain schema object** that validates the output shape — one where `.parse()` and `.encode()` are identical (no transformation).

My use case involves [nestjs-zod](https://github.com/BenLorantfy/nestjs-zod) and response validation with `ZodSerializerDto`. nestjs-zod can operate in two modes: by default it calls `.parse()` on the response, or with `codec: true` it calls `.encode()`. Neither works for my case.

Here's the setup. I have a codec-based schema describing data that arrives from an external service in encoded form:

```ts
const isoDate = z.codec(z.iso.datetime(), z.date(), {
  decode: (s) => new Date(s),
  encode: (d) => d.toISOString(),
});

const UserSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string(),
  createdAt: isoDate,
  updatedAt: isoDate.optional(),
});
```

In my NestJS controller, I fetch data from the external service, decode it, and then want to return the decoded form as the response. I want response validation to confirm the object matches the output shape before sending it:

```ts
class UserResponseDto extends createZodDto(UserSchema) {}
// or: createZodDto(UserSchema, { codec: true })

@Controller('users')
export class UserController {
  @Get(':id')
  @ZodSerializerDto(UserResponseDto)
  async getUser(@Param('id') id: string) {
    const raw = await this.externalService.getUser(id);  // returns encoded form
    const user = UserSchema.decode(raw);                  // decode to output form

    // At this point, `user` is { name: string, createdAt: Date, ... }
    // I want to validate and return it as-is.
    return user;
  }
}
```

The problem is that `ZodSerializerDto` will either:
- Call `.parse(user)` (default) — fails because `.parse()` expects the input types (ISO string, not `Date`)
- Call `.encode(user)` (`codec: true`) — transforms the data back to the input representation (Date → ISO string), which isn't what I want — I want to return the decoded object

What I actually need is a plain schema for the output shape — one I can pass to `ZodSerializerDto` that will validate `{ name: string, createdAt: Date, ... }` without transforming it.

For a single codec this is trivial — just use the output schema directly. But when codecs are spread across a larger composed schema, manual reconstruction duplicates the shape and defeats the purpose of composition:

```ts
const UserSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string(),
  createdAt: isoDate,
  updatedAt: isoDate.optional(),
  metadata: jsonMetadata,
  scores: z.array(z.object({
    category: z.string(),
    value: z.codec(z.string(), z.number(), {
      decode: (s) => Number(s),
      encode: (n) => String(n),
    }),
  })),
});

// ❌ No way to get a plain schema for the output shape of UserSchema
// without manually rebuilding the entire structure
```

`toJSONSchema(schema, { io: "output" })` already walks the tree resolving codecs to their output schemas, but produces JSON Schema — not a zod schema.

This is closely related to #5377 (and draft PR #5704), which describe the same underlying problem — needing to validate already-decoded data against the output side of a composed codec schema. The difference is that instead of a `reparse()` method as proposed there, I think a `toOutputSchema()` function makes more sense: it gives you a schema object you can pass to any framework, not just a one-off parse call.

## Proposed solution

A standalone `toOutputSchema(schema)` function that recursively walks a composed schema and resolves every pipe/codec to its output schema, returning a new plain zod schema (no codec transformations):

```ts
import { toOutputSchema } from "zod/v4";

const UserOutputSchema = toOutputSchema(UserSchema);
// A plain schema where:
//   createdAt expects Date (not ISO string)
//   scores[].value expects number (not string)
//   all non-codec fields are unchanged
//   .parse() and .encode() are identical — no transformations

class UserResponseDto extends createZodDto(UserOutputSchema) {}
```

The function recursively walks the schema tree, resolving each codec to its output schema. Non-codec parts of the tree are left unchanged — if there are no codecs in the subtree, the same schema instance is returned.

## Questions

Would a standalone `toOutputSchema()` function (following the pattern of `toJSONSchema()`) make sense as a built-in feature?

Zod should support a way to mutate input in a non-type-transforming way. This would differ from .transform(), which lets you transform the input at will and return a value of any type. A proposed mutate method would return an instance of the same class, instead of a ZodTransform.

z.string().mutate(val => {
   return val.startsWith("http") ? val : `http://${val}`
}) // => returns a ZodString instance

Because .transform() returns a ZodTransform and not an instance of the same class, I have been putting data initialization code in .superRefine(), but I know that is an anti-pattern and may not work with a future release of zod. I have many zod objects which extend a base zod object (where the initialization code would be). I don't want to remember to call .transform() on each and every final zod object that extends the base.

The idea of a mutate method was first mention by @colinhacks in issue #3323 on March 12, 2024.

## Summary

The top-level `zod/mini` subpath produces a namespace that TypeScript sees as completely empty (`keyof typeof z` resolves to `never`), so neither `import { z } from 'zod/mini'` nor `import * as z from 'zod/mini'` works. The deep path `zod/v4/mini` works fine despite having a structurally identical `.d.cts`.

## Environment

- `zod@4.4.3` (latest)
- `typescript@6.0.3`
- `tsconfig`: `"module": "esnext"`, `"moduleResolution": "bundler"`, `"target": "ES2024"`, `"erasableSyntaxOnly": true`
- Resolver picks `node_modules/zod/mini/index.d.cts` (CJS types via `types` condition while `import` points at ESM `.js`)

## Reproduction

```ts
// Fails: TS2305 — Module '"zod/mini"' has no exported member 'z'
import { z } from 'zod/mini';

// Fails: every member access errors — Property 'unknown' / 'number' / 'superRefine' /
// 'meta' / 'email' / 'prefault' / 'flattenError' / 'globalRegistry' / etc.
// does not exist on type 'typeof import("zod/mini")'
import * as zStar from 'zod/mini';
const a = zStar.unknown();
const b = zStar.number();

// Also fails: TS2694 — Namespace '"zod/mini"' has no exported member 'ZodMiniType' / 'core'
type T = zStar.ZodMiniType;

// Sanity check — keyof confirms the namespace is empty to TS:
type Keys = keyof typeof zStar; // → never

// Works perfectly with the deep path:
import * as z from 'zod/v4/mini';
const c = z.unknown();
const d = z.number();
type U = z.ZodMiniType;
type V = z.core.$ZodType;
```

## Observation

The two `.d.cts` files are byte-for-byte identical except for the relative path:

```ts
// node_modules/zod/mini/index.d.cts
import * as z from "../v4/mini/external.cjs";
export * from "../v4/mini/external.cjs";
export { z };

// node_modules/zod/v4/mini/index.d.cts
import * as z from "./external.cjs";
export * from "./external.cjs";
export { z };
```

Both point at the same `external.cjs`. But TS6 + `moduleResolution: bundler` only resolves the re-exports correctly for the deep path. Tracing the resolver shows it picks `index.d.cts` (CJS types) for both via the package.json `types` condition; the difference must be in how the bundler resolver handles `../v4/mini/external.cjs` crossing the inner `mini/package.json` boundary (which has `"type": "module"`).

## Workaround

Use `import * as z from 'zod/v4/mini'` instead of `'zod/mini'`. The zod.dev "[For library authors](https://zod.dev/library-authors)" guide already calls out `zod/v4/mini` as the canonical Mini entry point, so this is the documented stable path. But the top-level `./mini` shortcut documented elsewhere in the README does not work for TS consumers in this configuration.

## Related

- #5274 — "The types are wrong: Masquerading as CJS" (closed not-planned). Same family — zod 4.x ships only `.d.cts` under `"type": "module"`. This issue extends that: the `./mini` shortcut specifically is broken even in `bundler` resolution, while `./v4/mini` works.
- #3944 — `moduleResolution: NodeNext` failures (closed)
- #5072, #4879, #5573 — other broken subpath/exports symptoms

## Suggested fix

Make `mini/index.d.cts` use the same one-level-deep relative path style that `v4/mini/index.d.cts` uses (i.e., resolve to a sibling `external.cjs` rather than crossing the `mini/` ↔ `v4/mini/` boundary). Or update the README to direct users to `zod/v4/mini` rather than `zod/mini`.

## Versions

- zod: `4.3.6`
- typescript: `5.9.3`
- node: `22.22.1`

## Summary

The v4 getter pattern correctly handles mutual recursion when variants are combined with `z.union`, but **fails** with `z.discriminatedUnion` — TS emits `ts(7022)` / `ts(2615)` and the resulting type resolves to `any`.

The User/Post recursive example from the docs works because there's no union. Once a union is introduced over variants that recurse back through it via getter, only `z.union` produces a usable inferred type; `z.discriminatedUnion` does not.

## Reproduction

### ✅ Works — plain `z.union`

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

const variantA = z.object({
    kind: z.literal('a'),
    get child() { return tree; }
});

const variantB = z.object({
    kind: z.literal('b'),
    get sibling() { return tree; }
});

const tree = z.union([variantA, variantB]);

type Tree = z.input<typeof tree>;
//   ^? { kind: 'a'; child: ... | { kind: 'b'; sibling: ... } }
//      | { kind: 'b'; sibling: { kind: 'a'; child: ... } | ... }
```

Type is fully typed and recursive. ✓

### ❌ Broken — `z.discriminatedUnion`

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

const variantA = z.object({
    kind: z.literal('a'),
    get child() { return tree; }
});

const variantB = z.object({
    kind: z.literal('b'),
    get sibling() { return tree; }
});

const tree = z.discriminatedUnion('kind', [variantA, variantB]);

type Tree = z.input<typeof tree>;
//   ^? any
```

Diagnostics:

- `ts(7022)`: `'tree' implicitly has type 'any' because it does not have a type annotation and is referenced directly or indirectly in its own initializer.`
- `ts(2615)`: `Type of property 'child' circularly references itself in mapped type ...`
- `ts(2615)`: `Type of property 'sibling' circularly references itself in mapped type ...`

Adding explicit getter return type annotations (`get child(): typeof tree`) doesn't help — TS still reports `ts(2502)` (`'child' is referenced directly or indirectly in its own type annotation.`).

## Expected behavior

`z.discriminatedUnion` should infer the same recursive shape as `z.union` does.

## Likely cause

`ZodDiscriminatedUnion`'s type computation eagerly maps over variants to build the discriminator → branch lookup, requiring every variant's shape to be fully resolved before the union's type. Plain `ZodUnion` is lazier — it just unions the variants' inferred types, which TS handles through normal lazy expansion.

## Workaround

For now, use `z.union` instead of `z.discriminatedUnion` for recursive schemas, accepting the runtime parse-perf loss (no discriminator short-circuit), or fall back to v3's `z.lazy` + `z.ZodType<X>` cast pattern with a hand-written recursive type.

