# Bug
Since zod 4.4.1, all validation error messages in esbuild-bundled (ESM) applications fall back to the generic ["Invalid input"](vscode-file://vscode-app/Applications/Visual%20Studio%20Code.app/Contents/Resources/app/out/vs/code/electron-browser/workbench/workbench.html) instead of descriptive locale messages like 'Invalid option: expected one of "km"|"mi"'.

# Root Cause
zod/v4/package.json was updated in 4.4.1 to include "sideEffects": false. This causes esbuild to tree-shake the [config(en())](vscode-file://vscode-app/Applications/Visual%20Studio%20Code.app/Contents/Resources/app/out/vs/code/electron-browser/workbench/workbench.html) call in zod/v4/classic/external.js — the call that registers the English locale.

Without the locale registered, all errors fall through to the hardcoded fallback in zod/v4/core/util.js:

`message = config.localeError?.(iss) ?? "Invalid input"`

# Reproduction
Minimal self-contained test (requires esbuild and zod@>=4.4.1):
```typescript
import { execSync } from "node:child_process";
import { mkdtempSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { build } from "esbuild";

/**
 * Self-contained reproduction test for zod locale tree-shaking bug.
 *
 * Since zod 4.4.1, `zod/v4/package.json` declares `"sideEffects": false`.
 * This causes esbuild to tree-shake the `config(en())` call in
 * `zod/v4/classic/external.js` that initializes the English locale.
 *
 * Without the locale, all zod validation error messages fall back to
 * the generic "Invalid input" instead of descriptive messages like:
 *   'Invalid option: expected one of "km"|"mi"'
 *
 * The fallback happens here in zod's core/util.js:
 *   message = config.localeError?.(iss) ?? "Invalid input"
 */
describe("zod v4 locale tree-shaking bug (esbuild reproduction)", () => {
    let tmpDir: string;

    beforeAll(() => {
        tmpDir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "zod-locale-bug-"));
        // Install zod 4.4.1 (the version that introduced sideEffects: false)
        writeFileSync(join(tmpDir, "package.json"), JSON.stringify({ type: "module" }));
        execSync("npm install zod@4.4.1 --silent", { cwd: tmpDir, stdio: "pipe" });
        // With 4.3.6 it works
        // execSync("npm install zod@4.3.6 --silent", { cwd: tmpDir, stdio: "pipe" });
    });

    afterAll(() => {
        rmSync(tmpDir, { recursive: true, force: true });
    });

    const bundleAndRun = async (sourceCode: string): Promise<string> => {
        const inputFile = join(tmpDir, "input.mjs");
        const outputFile = join(tmpDir, "output.mjs");

        writeFileSync(inputFile, sourceCode);

        await build({
            entryPoints: [inputFile],
            bundle: true,
            format: "esm",
            platform: "node",
            outfile: outputFile,
        });

        const result = execSync(`node ${outputFile}`, {
            stdio: "pipe",
            encoding: "utf-8",
        });

        return result.trim();
    };

    it("enum validation error message is 'Invalid input' instead of descriptive message due to locale tree-shaking", async () => {
        const source = `
            import * as z from "zod/v4";
            const schema = z.enum(["km", "mi"]);
            const result = schema.safeParse("feet");
            if (!result.success) {
                console.log(JSON.stringify({
                    message: result.error.issues[0].message,
                    code: result.error.issues[0].code,
                }));
            }
        `;

        const output = JSON.parse(await bundleAndRun(source));

        // BUG: esbuild tree-shakes the locale, so we get generic "Invalid input"
        // instead of the descriptive 'Invalid option: expected one of "km"|"mi"'
        expect(output.code).toBe("invalid_value");
        // expect(output.message).toBe("Invalid input");
        // This is what the message SHOULD be:
        expect(output.message).toBe('Invalid option: expected one of "km"|"mi"');
    });

    it("workaround: explicit locale import preserves descriptive messages", async () => {
        const source = `
            import { config } from "zod/v4/core";
            import en from "zod/v4/locales/en.js";
            config(en());

            import * as z from "zod/v4";
            const schema = z.enum(["km", "mi"]);
            const result = schema.safeParse("feet");
            if (!result.success) {
                console.log(JSON.stringify({
                    message: result.error.issues[0].message,
                    code: result.error.issues[0].code,
                }));
            }
        `;

        const output = JSON.parse(await bundleAndRun(source));

        // With explicit locale import, the descriptive message works
        expect(output.code).toBe("invalid_value");
        expect(output.message).toBe('Invalid option: expected one of "km"|"mi"');
    });
});
```

# Expected behaviour
Invalid option: expected one of "km"|"mi"
# Actual Behavior
Invalid input

# Workaround
Explicitly import and configure the locale:
```typescript
import { config } from "zod/v4/core";
import en from "zod/v4/locales/en.js";
config(en());
```

Disclaimer: The issue was created using help of AI but I was able to confirming that reverting back to zod version 4.3.6 fixed the issue. When I tried writing a unit test without bundling I had always the english expected message locally, the self contained test which was created by the AI made it clear that it is indeed a combination of changed zod behaviour when using esbuild.

`z.string().cidrv6()` correctly validates IPv6 CIDRs at runtime using `new URL()`, but the `pattern` emitted by `z.toJSONSchema()` rejects many valid inputs.

**Affected version:** 4.4.3 (current main)

## What happens

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

// Runtime validation — correct
z.string().cidrv6().safeParse("2001:db8::/32").success;    // true
z.string().cidrv6().safeParse("2001:db8:1::/48").success;  // true

// JSON schema — incorrect pattern
z.toJSONSchema(z.string().cidrv6());
// {
//   "$schema": "...",
//   "type": "string",
//   "format": "cidrv6",
//   "pattern": "^(([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4}:){7}...|([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4})?::([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4}:?){0,6})\\/..."
// }
```

The exported `pattern` — `regexes.cidrv6` in `src/v4/core/regexes.ts` — only allows **0 or 1 groups before `::`** via `([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4})?::`. Valid CIDRs with 2+ groups before `::` are not matched:

| Input | Zod runtime | JSON schema `pattern` |
|-------|-------------|----------------------|
| `2001:db8::/32` | ✅ valid | ❌ no match |
| `2001:db8:1::/48` | ✅ valid | ❌ no match |
| `2001:0db8:85a3::/64` | ✅ valid | ❌ no match |
| `fe80::/10` | ✅ valid | ✅ matches |
| `::/0` | ✅ valid | ✅ matches |

`2001:db8::/32` is the canonical documentation prefix (RFC 3849) and appears in virtually every IPv6 tutorial and RFC example.

## Why it matters

The `pattern` field is consumed by OpenAPI validators, API gateways, and frontend validation libraries. Any system that applies the Zod-generated JSON schema directly will reject addresses that Zod itself accepts, silently breaking interoperability.

## Root cause

`$ZodCIDRv6` correctly overrides `inst._zod.check` to use `new URL()`, but `def.pattern` is still set to the incomplete `regexes.cidrv6` before calling `$ZodStringFormat.init()`. `$ZodCheckStringFormat.init()` then adds that pattern to `bag.patterns`, which `stringProcessor` in `json-schema-processors.ts` emits as the `pattern` field.

The runtime check and the schema metadata diverge at `src/v4/core/schemas.ts:885`:

```ts
def.pattern ??= regexes.cidrv6; // not used for validation
```

The comment acknowledges the disconnect but the pattern still leaks into the JSON schema output.

## Possible fixes

1. **Fix `regexes.cidrv6`** to correctly cover all compressed forms (similar to how `regexes.ipv6` enumerates eight alternatives).
2. **Don't set `def.pattern`** for `$ZodCIDRv6` at all, so no `pattern` is emitted in the JSON schema. Validators that need a regex would have to rely on `format: "cidrv6"`.

The [release notes](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/releases/tag/v4.4.0) for Zod 4.4.0 include the following code example for [codec inversion](https://zod.dev/codecs?id=inverting-codecs#inverting-codecs):

```ts
